{
  "requirement": "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:\n\n:::{sites} skills\n```yaml\nlanguage: markdown\npaths: [assets/skills]\nrule:\n  kind: minus_metadata\nmatch:\n  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"\nno_match:\n  - \"# Example\\n\"\n```\n:::",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** The requirement has two parts.\n- The \"MAY\" clause, which allows links to specialized material, imposes nothing to check.\n- The \"MUST\" clause says each such link states when to read the file. Each bundled skill upholds this on its own, so it is a site obligation.\n\n**Site query `requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references/skills`.**\n- I ran it. It returns exactly five files: the `SKILL.md` of `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence`, `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` and `habeas-refactor`. These are all the bundled skills under `assets/skills`. They match the `SKILLS` constant in `tests/authoring_cli.rs`.\n- The reference files under `references/` have no front matter, so the `minus_metadata` rule does not select them. Searching `assets/skills` for other links to `.md` files found only the `references/...` pointers. The `SKILL.md` front matter is the one construct that identifies a skill, and the query selects it.\n- The match snippet (front matter followed by `# Example`) shows what it selects. The no_match snippet (a heading with no front matter) shows what it excludes. I found no site missed or wrongly selected.\n- The requirement defines no violation queries, so there is nothing to assess there.\n\n**Test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]`.** It is marked `{test}` for the subject. Its coverage of the \"say when to read\" obligation:\n- **`SKILL.md` links.** The `links` table pins the exact sentence in each skill's `SKILL.md` that gives the reading condition. These cover the `habeas`, `habeas-requirements`, `habeas-evidence` and `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skills. A loop asserts each sentence is present after whitespace collapse.\n- **Reference-file links.** The `reference_guidance` table does the same for pointers inside reference files (`review-prompt.md`, `reviews.md`, `distributed-requirements.md`).\n- **Every pointer is guarded.** A regex finds every `references/*.md` pointer, whether a markdown link or inline code, in every file of every skill from `skill_files`. The test asserts each pointer lies inside one of the checked guidance sentences. A new or reworded pointer with no recorded guidance therefore fails.\n- **No unchecked reference file.** For each skill, every reference file listed by `skill_files` must have at least one entry in `links`, and its sentence must be present in `SKILL.md`.\n- **`habeas-refactor`.** It has no reference files or pointers, so there is nothing to test for it.\n- **Reference-file list.** `skill_files` lists every file under each skill's `references/` directory, as confirmed with `list_files` and the `include_str!` search.\n\nThe test asserts presence of curated sentences. It does not judge in code whether a sentence states a condition. That judgment is made once, when the sentences are curated, and the test enforces that every pointer stays tied to one of them. This is adequate for the requirement.\n\nI found no gaps and there are no acknowledgments.",
  "references": "5b5a1f7cf171bc78f06adc8576f6e3e3dcb20c5f062af5c778e2b34a76dbfa84",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references",
      "matches": [
        "requirements.cli.skill_conventions.references"
      ],
      "text": [
        "::::{req} skill_conventions.references",
        "A bundled skill MAY put material needed only in specialized situations in files it links to. Each such link MUST say when to read the file. Each bundled skill, recognized by its front matter, is a site:",
        "",
        ":::{sites} skills",
        "```yaml",
        "language: markdown",
        "paths: [assets/skills]",
        "rule:",
        "  kind: minus_metadata",
        "match:",
        "  - \"---\\nname: example\\n---\\n\\n# Example\\n\"",
        "no_match:",
        "  - \"# Example\\n\"",
        "```",
        ":::",
        "::::"
      ]
    },
